  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,\u22a5\\,w.$ Find $(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+5\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & +(1\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5+4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(6-4-5-6)+(3+3+10-8) \\\\  & =-9+8 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
